# How git should treat line endings in this repository.
#
# The problem this solves: by default git asks your *machine's* config
# (core.autocrlf) whether to rewrite line endings. That setting is personal and
# is not shared, so two people can clone the same repo and get different bytes.
#
# The rules below pin the behaviour into the repo itself:
#   1. Every text file is stored as LF (canonical) - no whole-file diff noise.
#   2. Files that Windows tooling runs are checked out as CRLF, because cmd.exe
#      and the PowerShell 5.1 host expect CRLF.
# This is a Windows-only project (native tray app, .cmd/.ps1 launchers), so
# CRLF on checkout is correct for every file type here.

* text=auto

# Launchers and scripts executed by Windows.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Sources built on Windows.
*.cpp text eol=crlf
*.h   text eol=crlf
*.svg text eol=crlf

# Documentation is read everywhere; LF keeps it portable.
*.md  text eol=lf

# Generated binaries - never rewrite these, they are not text.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.exe binary
